ing's (x 723 px against the window's 708), the draughtsman's perspective converging faster than the camera's (the solve's own note), so the pair is centred under the window above, 0.8 m either side. Their glazing bars and the bow light's iron are K06's and K08's, RECONSTRUCTED (L-k01-1637-basement-lights-2535). T-2537: the wing's second-storey window is the engraving's pair, read off the print itself: its dark opening spans x 675-745 px (70 px; the build's 1.1 m sash spanned 688-730, 42 px, so about 38 px a metre on this face through the camera, and 70 px is about 1.83 m), a pale bar down its middle at x 704-707 px, and in each half a meeting rail and no bars, so two one-over-one sashes. It is built as K06's paired sash, 1.8 m clear, two 0.88 m lights on a 0.04 m mullion; through the camera it now spans x 675-743 px. The mullion's width is reconstructed (L-k01-1637-wing-pair-2537): the print draws it as a single 3 px line, as it draws every bar, and the kit's 0.2 m stone mullion stood as an 8 px brick pier between two holes where the print draws one opening. Its height was read and left: the right light is dark from y 353 to 445 px on the print and on the build alike, so the 2.1 m sash at 0.75 m over the floor stands. T-2780: the south wall's canted bay (`south_flank`), which the c. 1870 Grosvenor photograph shows and the 1911 sheet still draws, so the remodel kept it (the photograph's match record weighs that). Its plan is the sheet's (s20fp-1637-b1: junctions at x 1427.06 and 1430.61 m in the trace frame, u 4.34 to 0.79, so 3.55 m at the wall centred at s 8.435 m on K01's side.north wall, which on this east-side house is its south flank; cheeks at 45 degrees and its front 2.22 m, 0.75 m out). Its heights are the photograph's, read through T-2755's camera at about 57 px a metre on the bay's front: the sill band at about y 680 px (3.1 m; the base built to 2.95 m and the lights' sills 0.2 m over it, which K08's frames need), the front's arched light to its crown at y